--- body ---
## Context

Found during the `/dream -home-haduong--claude` run of 2026-09-16.

`skills/dream/read-index.py` parses `MEMORY.md` with

    re.match(r"-\s+\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^\)]+)\)\s*[—-]?\s*(.*)", line)

`[^\]]+` stops at the first `]`, so a line whose **title** contains a closing
bracket fails to match and is skipped. There is no warning: the entry simply
does not appear in the JSON the rest of dream reasons over.

One entry is affected today:

    - [grep -E is line-oriented; bash [[ =~ ]] matches the whole string](feedback_grep_line_vs_bash_wholestring.md)

The run read 118 entries against 119 index lines. Confirmed with a positive
control: a plain title matches, `- [A test [green] for a reason](x.md)` is
skipped.

## Why this is not cosmetic

Dream's step 6 rewrites `MEMORY.md` from the entries it parsed. A run that
regenerates the index from 118 parsed entries **deletes the 119th line**. The
harness has already measured that no recall channel fires on this runtime — the
resident index is the only door (`reference_no_recall_channel_fires`), so an
entry dropped from the index is not demoted, it is unreachable. The consolidation
pass would consume a memory by accident, report `119 → 118` as a successful
dedup, and nothing downstream could tell that from an intended deletion.

The 2026-09-16 run avoided this only because the count mismatch was noticed and
the index was rebuilt from a bracket-safe parse by hand.

The same blindness affects the passes that read the parsed set rather than the
file: an unparsed entry is invisible to classification, to reflection, and to
step 7's provenance `record`, so its `last_confirmed` freezes and the decay pass
will eventually flag it for a staleness it never had.

## Relevant files

- `skills/dream/read-index.py` — the regex
- `skills/dream/SKILL.md` — steps 1, 2, 6, 7 all consume its output
- `projects/-home-haduong--claude/memory/feedback_grep_line_vs_bash_wholestring.md`
  — the live instance
- `projects/*/memory/MEMORY.md` — 46 indexes to check for other bracketed titles

## Actions

1. Anchor the title capture so it cannot end early: match greedily up to the
   final `](...)` on the line, e.g. `^-\s+\[(.+)\]\(([^)]+)\)`. Any fix must be
   checked against a title containing `]`, `]]`, and a title containing `)`.
2. Make the parser **loud rather than lossy**: count index lines that look like
   pointers and fail to parse, and refuse to proceed when the number is nonzero.
   A silent skip is what turned a parser bug into a deletion risk.
3. Sweep all 46 project indexes for titles carrying brackets and report them.
4. Check whether `skills/dream/commit.py` or the provenance scripts share the
   same pattern.

## Test

- Positive control first: a line with a plain title parses. Then a line whose
  title contains `]]` parses, and its filename is captured correctly.
- A malformed pointer line makes the reader exit non-zero rather than return a
  short list.
- An end-to-end guard: parsed-entry count equals pointer-line count for every
  project index in the repo.

## Verification

- [ ] A bracketed title round-trips through read, classify and rewrite unchanged
- [ ] An unparseable pointer line fails loudly instead of being skipped
- [ ] Parsed count equals pointer count across all project indexes, asserted by test
- [ ] No other dream script carries the same regex shape

## Exit criteria

- [ ] No memory entry can be removed from an index by a parser that could not read it
